From e02e807d89d5dcbe2cce127e89b7ed32ec574ada Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:59:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Settle item 1-1's re-entry residual, and fix the comparison
 that caused most of it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

0105 reported 84.1% of re-entered bodies reproducing the eager tree and said
plainly what that number was worth: a printed diff answers only "equal up to a
renaming somebody thought of in advance", and the residual 15.9% looked like
ordinal divergence on every instance examined without that being proof. This
answers it, and the first thing it found was a defect in the comparison.

The gensym families were canonicalised in one pass whose ordinal table was keyed
on the bare number, so Context3 and #TempJSValue3 shared an entry — and any
function where two families drew the same number on one side and not the other
desynchronised every ordinal after it. One table per family takes five of six
corpora from 96.6-99.9% to 100.0%.

Then the residual is partitioned rather than described. Beside the equality up
to an order-preserving renaming, the same three families are ERASED instead of
mapped, which asks whether the trees agree in every token a counter did not
produce. That is 5 723 of 5 723 on every corpus, with 0 throwing — so no
re-entered body differs from its eager tree in a node, an operator, a constant
or a shape. Erasure is the weaker question and is reported beside the stronger
rather than instead of it.

What is left is 471 functions whose counter ordinals differ, and they classify
exactly two ways with nothing in "other". 460 are the site table's -1: the check
compiles every body a second time and a retained context recompiles its whole
subtree, so Typescript drives the process-wide counter from 24 759 to exactly
its 65 536 cap. 11 are the eager side reusing a site the re-entry allocated
fresh, which is 4-2b's tier-2 rule working as designed — a recompile for tiering
re-uses the tier-1 site so the feedback it consumes stays addressable, and a
re-entry has no tier-1 tree to re-use from. Both are properties of compiling the
same body twice in one process, which a deferral by construction does not do.

Per-process, so the corpora do not share the counter: closure 100.0%, mandreel
100.0%, jquery 99.8%, pdfjs 99.7%, box2d 99.7%, typescript 73.7% — 91.8% over
5 723, with every one of the remaining 471 accounted for.

Both equalities are pinned directly rather than trusted for never having failed:
one fixture drives a counter renaming (both accept), a token no counter produced
(both reject), and a site reused on one side only — which the strong equality
reports and the weak one cannot, which is exactly why the residual is classified
from the raw sequences instead of left inside the weaker number.
